>> title: Ham Hock Stock, Instant Pot
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, stock, southern, smoked, instant pot, pressure cooker, make-ahead
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot ham hock stock, pressure cooker ham hock broth, instant pot pot likker, smoked pork stock, smoked pork pot, seasoning meat, smoked turkey stock
>> servings: 12
>> dish: ham-hock-stock
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> pairs-with: collard-greens, green-beans, black-eyed-peas, butter-beans
>> time: 1 hr 30 min
>> step.1: rinse, into the pot with the aromatics
>> step.2: pressure cook 45 min
>> step.3: natural release 20 min
>> step.4: strain, pick the meat off the bone, salt at the end

Rinse @smoked ham hocks{2}(about 2 lb / 900 g), @smoked pork neck bones{1%lb}(450 g) and lay them in the #Instant Pot{} with @cold water{2 1/2%qt}(2.4 L), @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @garlic{6%cloves}(smashed), @bay leaves{2}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp} and @dried red pepper flakes{1/2%tsp}. Two and a half quarts rather than four: pressure gives nothing back to evaporation, so a stovetop fill line here makes weak stock.

Lock the lid on @&(~1)pot{} and ~pressure cook{45%min} at high pressure. Forty-five minutes is what a smoked hock takes to give up its collagen and let the meat come off the bone in the pot — the same forty-five minutes whether the hock is small or large, because it is the connective tissue setting the clock, not the weight.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{20%min} until the pin drops. Do not vent it: the fat is emulsified through the liquid while it is hot, and a hard release keeps it there instead of letting it rise.

Strain @&(~1)pot{} through a #fine sieve{} and pick the meat off the bone. Stir the picked meat back into whichever pot this stock goes into. Salt is for the end, not now, because the hocks are already salty and this comes out more concentrated than the three-hour version, not less. It is smokier and less clear — three hours of open simmer mellows the smoke and this does not — so taste it before you build on it.
